CLO Chakra Program

Hedge Allocation – Data Flow and Data Model Design

1. Overview

The CLO Chakra Program manages hedge supply requests for loans and allocates them to CLO programs within a replenishment cycle.

The system integrates with the following upstream systems:

Loan Inventory System – provides loan attributes

Hedge Request System – provides hedge supply requests

Porting Service – loads inventory datasets

Camunda Workflow Engine – orchestrates data refresh and lifecycle events

Camunda Rule Engine – evaluates allocation rules


The CLO system maintains its own hedge dataset and allocation ledger, while loan inventory remains owned by the Loan Inventory system.


---

2. System Components

The following data sources and services participate in the flow.

Component	Responsibility

Porting Service	loads loan inventory datasets
Loan Inventory DB	stores daily and monthly inventory tables
Hedge Application DB	stores hedge requests
Camunda Workflow	orchestrates periodic data synchronization
CLO Service	processes hedge requests and performs allocations
CLO Database	stores hedge requests and allocation ledger
Camunda Rule Engine	runs rule validations



---

3. Loan Inventory Data Model

The Loan Inventory system exposes two datasets.

Table	Purpose

LOAN_INVENTORY_DAILY	current loan attributes
LOAN_INVENTORY_MONTHLY	frozen monthly snapshot


Inventory Data Load

The Porting Service loads these tables:

Daily load:

truncate + load
loan_inventory_daily

Monthly load:

truncate + load
loan_inventory_monthly

These datasets remain the source of truth for loan attributes.

The CLO system does not duplicate these attributes.


---

4. CLO Loan Inventory Access

The CLO system accesses inventory through a view abstraction.

CLO_LOAN_INVENTORY_VIEW

This view dynamically references the appropriate dataset depending on cycle phase.

Before Static Freeze

loan_inventory_daily

After Static Freeze

loan_inventory_monthly

This ensures:

UI displays correct inventory

Camunda rule engine evaluates frozen data

no application logic change is required



---

5. Hedge Request Source

Hedge supply requests originate in the Hedge Application database.

Example table:

HEDGE_REQUEST

The CLO system maintains its own hedge table:

CLO_HEDGE

This table contains:

hedge attributes from the source system

CLO-specific attributes used during allocation



---

6. Daily Camunda Synchronization Flow

Camunda orchestrates a daily synchronization workflow.

This flow ingests new hedge requests and enriches them with loan attributes.

Step 1 – Camunda Trigger

Camunda invokes a daily synchronization process within CLO.

Purpose:

load latest inventory

fetch new hedge requests

update CLO hedge dataset



---

Step 2 – Read Loan Inventory

CLO reads the latest inventory from:

loan_inventory_daily

This provides current loan attributes.


---

Step 3 – Identify New Hedge Requests

CLO queries the Hedge system for new requests.

Condition:

created_date > latest created_date in clo_hedge

Only new hedge requests are fetched.

Example logic:

SELECT *
FROM hedge_request
WHERE created_date >
(
SELECT MAX(created_date)
FROM clo_hedge
)


---

Step 4 – Merge Hedge and Loan Data

The new hedge requests are joined with loan inventory to enrich them with loan attributes.

Example merge logic:

SELECT
h.hedge_id,
h.loan_id,
h.req_type,
h.reqd_hedge_amt,
l.rating,
l.industry,
l.country
FROM hedge_request h
JOIN loan_inventory_daily l
ON h.loan_id = l.loan_id
WHERE h.status = 'OPEN'

This produces an enriched hedge dataset.


---

Step 5 – Upsert Into CLO_HEDGE

The enriched records are inserted or updated in the CLO hedge table.

Example upsert logic:

MERGE INTO clo_hedge t
USING source_data s
ON (t.hedge_id = s.hedge_id)

WHEN MATCHED THEN
UPDATE SET
reqd_hedge_amt = s.reqd_hedge_amt,
loan_rating = s.rating

WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
INSERT
(
hedge_id,
loan_id,
req_type,
reqd_hedge_amt,
loan_rating,
created_cycle_id
)
VALUES
(
s.hedge_id,
s.loan_id,
s.req_type,
s.reqd_hedge_amt,
s.rating,
:cycle_id
);

This ensures that CLO_HEDGE remains synchronized with the Hedge system.


---

7. CLO Hedge Data Model

The CLO_HEDGE table stores hedge requests enriched with CLO attributes.

Example attributes:

Column	Description

hedge_id	hedge request identifier
loan_id	underlying loan
req_type	hedge request type
reqd_hedge_amt	requested hedge amount
created_cycle_id	cycle in which hedge was created
allocation_status	allocation state
deadline_date	hedge deadline


Loan attributes are not permanently stored; they are referenced from inventory views when needed.


---

8. Hedge Allocation Model

Hedge allocations are stored in a ledger table.

CLO_HEDGE_ALLOCATIONS

This table records allocation transactions.

Characteristics:

insert-only

immutable history

no update or delete operations


Example transaction types:

Type	Description

ALLOCATE	new allocation
REALLOCATE	move allocation between programs
REDUCE	decrease allocation
DROP	reverse allocations when loan removed


Final allocation values are calculated using aggregation.


---

9. Hedge Supply Freeze

During each replenishment cycle a freeze event occurs.

The freeze process consists of three steps.

Step 1 – Stop New Hedge Requests

Cycle status is updated.

REQUEST_STOPPED

CLO rejects new hedge requests for the cycle.


---

Step 2 – Snapshot Hedge Supply

Current hedge supply is captured into a pool table.

CLO_CYCLE_HEDGE_POOL

Example logic:

INSERT INTO clo_cycle_hedge_pool
SELECT
cycle_id,
hedge_id,
reqd_hedge_amt
FROM clo_hedge
WHERE created_cycle_id = :cycle_id

This snapshot represents the maximum hedge supply available for allocation.


---

Step 3 – Freeze Loan Inventory

At the freeze point the inventory view switches from:

loan_inventory_daily

to

loan_inventory_monthly

All rule evaluations now use frozen loan attributes.


---

10. Rule Evaluation

Rules are executed by the Camunda Rule Engine.

Loan attributes used by rules are loaded from:

CLO_LOAN_INVENTORY_VIEW

Example rule query:

SELECT loan_id
FROM CLO_LOAN_INVENTORY_VIEW
WHERE rating < 'BB'

Rules determine whether a hedge allocation violates program constraints.


---

11. Cycle Closure

At the end of the cycle:

1. Camunda updates the cycle status to CLOSED


2. Allocation activity stops


3. Inventory view switches back to daily inventory


4. A new cycle begins




---

12. Benefits of the Design

Simplified Integration

Loan inventory is accessed directly from the source database without staging tables.


---

Controlled Data Synchronization

Camunda orchestrates the hedge ingestion workflow ensuring consistent updates.


---

Data Consistency

Loan inventory views ensure rule evaluations operate on stable datasets.


---

Full Allocation Auditability

The allocation ledger preserves a complete history of allocation transactions.


---

Flexible Allocation Management

Allocations can be rebalanced without modifying historical records.


